# GitHub secrets checklist — {{projectName}}

Every secret the workflows Runner Forge emitted for you actually reference, what
each one is, and where to get it.

Add them at
`https://github.com/{{githubOwner}}/{{githubRepo}}/settings/secrets/actions`.

> **None of these belongs in `forge.json`.** That file holds only non-secret
> identifiers. Runner Forge keeps the real values in your OS keystore — Windows
> Credential Manager or the macOS Keychain — and this checklist exists so the
> same values reach GitHub for the jobs that need them there.

Signing mode for this configuration: **`{{signingMode}}`**

---

## Required in every mode

| Secret | What it is | Where to get it | Held locally? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `PACE_ACCOUNT` | Your PACE/iLok account name. | iLok License Manager, or your PACE developer account. | {{havePaceAccount}} |
| `PACE_PASSWORD` | That account's password. | Same. | {{havePacePassword}} |

---

## Windows Authenticode — Azure Trusted Signing

Referenced by `sign-windows-artifact.ps1`. Set
`signing.windows.provider` to `none` if you do not sign Windows binaries; the
artifacts are still produced, just unsigned.

| Secret | What it is | Where to get it | Held locally? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `AZURE_CLIENT_ID` | Service principal application (client) ID. | Azure portal → App registrations. | {{haveAzureClientId}} |
| `AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET` | That service principal's secret. | Azure portal → App registrations → Certificates & secrets. | {{haveAzureClientSecret}} |
| `AZURE_TENANT_ID` | Your Entra tenant ID. | Azure portal → Microsoft Entra ID → Overview. | {{haveAzureTenantId}} |

The non-secret half — endpoint `{{azureEndpoint}}`, account `{{azureAccount}}`,
profile `{{azureProfile}}` — is baked into the workflow and is not a secret.

---

## macOS Developer ID, notarization and stapling

Referenced by `sign-macos-artifact.sh`.

| Secret | What it is | Where to get it | Held locally? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `APPLE_DEV_ID_P12` | Base64 of your Developer ID Application `.p12`. | Export from Keychain Access, then `base64 -i cert.p12 \| pbcopy`. | {{haveAppleP12}} |
| `APPLE_DEV_ID_P12_PASSWORD` | The password you set on that export. | You chose it during export. | {{haveAppleP12Password}} |
| `APPLE_ASC_ISSUER_ID` | App Store Connect API issuer ID. | App Store Connect → Users and Access → Integrations → Keys. | {{haveAscIssuerId}} |
| `APPLE_ASC_KEY_ID` | The API key's ID. | Same page, next to the key. | {{haveAscKeyId}} |
| `APPLE_ASC_PRIVATE_KEY` | Contents of the downloaded `AuthKey_XXXX.p8`. | Downloadable exactly once when the key is created. | {{haveAscPrivateKey}} |

The certificate *names* — `{{devIdAppIdentity}}` and
`{{devIdInstallerIdentity}}` — and the team ID `{{appleTeamId}}` are not
secrets and are baked into the workflow.

> **Both Apple certificates are needed and they are different.** Developer ID
> **Application** signs the plugin bundles; Developer ID **Installer** signs the
> `.pkg`. Using one where the other is required fails with a message that does
> not obviously say so.

---

## Not needed

| Secret | Why not |
|---|---|
| A runner registration token | Runner Forge mints a single-use JIT config from your GitHub App key immediately before each runner starts. There is no long-lived registration token anywhere in this product. |
| `GITHUB_TOKEN` | Provided automatically by Actions. The `release` job uses it via `${{ github.token }}`. |

---

## After adding them

Dispatch the build workflow once with `use_hosted_runners: true`. That exercises
the fallback path and confirms the secrets resolve, without depending on your
self-hosted runners being up. An untested fallback is not a fallback.
